 ### Depth-Based Gaussian Pruning
-#### definition
-- [S1] A pruning strategy that varies the pruning threshold based on the relative depth (z-axis value) of 3D Gaussians to preserve points at the far plane in forward-facing scenes.
-#### step
-- [S1] Determine the relative depth for each 3D Gaussian based on its z-axis value.
-- [S1] Scale the base pruning threshold $t_p$ by a multiplier $w_p$ determined by the relative depth. The lowest threshold is applied to the farthest point.
-#### parameter
-- [S1] $t_p$: The base 3D Gaussian pruning threshold. Value: $5 \times 10^{-3}$ for real defocus blur dataset; $1 \times 10^{-2}$ for real camera motion blur dataset.
-- [S1] $w_p$: The pruning threshold multiplier based on depth. Value: 3.
+- [S2] A pruning strategy that varies the pruning threshold based on the relative depth (z-axis value) of 3D Gaussians to preserve points at the far plane in forward-facing scenes.
+- [S2] Determine the relative depth for each 3D Gaussian based on its z-axis value.
+- [S2] Scale the base pruning threshold $t_p$ by a multiplier $w_p$ determined by the relative depth. The lowest threshold is applied to the farthest point.
+- [S2] $t_p$: The base 3D Gaussian pruning threshold. Value: $5 \times 10^{-3}$ for real defocus blur dataset; $1 \times 10^{-2}$ for real camera motion blur dataset.
+- [S2] $w_p$: The pruning threshold multiplier based on depth. Value: 3.
 
 ## Background
-The concept of pruning Gaussians to manage density was introduced in the original 3D Gaussian Splatting paper [14], which established opacity-based pruning as a standard component of adaptive density control. Subsequent research has refined this mechanism to address specific artifacts. For instance, [S1] (Deblurring 3D Gaussian Splatting) introduced depth-based pruning to compensate for sparsity in forward-facing scenes, ensuring that distant points are not erroneously removed due to lower opacity contributions. More recently, Principled Uncertainty Pruning [9680-principled-uncertainty-pruning] applied statistical learning theory (Fisher Information) to provide a theoretically grounded approach to model compression, moving beyond heuristic thresholds.
+The concept of adaptive density control, including pruning, was introduced in the original 3D Gaussian Splatting paper [14] (cited in [S2]) to manage the number of 3D Gaussians through periodic densification and pruning. Subsequent research has expanded on this foundation. For instance, [S1, unofficial] surveys various sparsification techniques, including GaussianSPA [86], which interleaves training with sparsification, and Speedy-Splat [85], which applies primitive-sparse selection. [S2] introduces depth-based pruning specifically for deblurring tasks in forward-facing scenes to compensate for sparsity at the far end of the scene.
 
